# ============================================
# WINDSURF PROJECT RULES — NESTJS TEMPLATE
# ============================================

# -------------------------------------------------
# 1. GLOBAL PROJECT ARCHITECTURE
# -------------------------------------------------
The project uses a Modular Monolith architecture designed for 
future microservices separation. All code MUST follow this structure:

src/
  modules/<feature>/
    dto/
    entities/
    <feature>.controller.ts
    <feature>.service.ts
    <feature>.module.ts

config/
common/
  decorators/
  guards/
  interceptors/
  pipes/
supabase-schema.sql
README.md
PROJECT_STRUCTURE.md

Rules:
- NEVER put business logic outside module services.
- NEVER share internal logic between modules.
- NEVER import a module's service into another module.
- ALWAYS use clients or shared interfaces for future microservices.
- ALWAYS follow strict separation: controller → service → DB.

# -------------------------------------------------
# 2. MODULE GENERATION RULES
# -------------------------------------------------
When creating a new module, Windsurf MUST generate:

modules/<feature>/
  entities/<entity>.entity.ts
  dto/create-<entity>.dto.ts
  dto/update-<entity>.dto.ts
  dto/<entity>-response.dto.ts
  <feature>.controller.ts
  <feature>.service.ts
  <feature>.module.ts

Rules:
- DTOs MUST use class-validator decorators.
- Entities MUST be TypeScript interfaces (not classes).
- Services MUST contain all business logic.
- Controllers MUST only contain HTTP routing logic.
- Modules MUST export services only when required.

# -------------------------------------------------
# 3. DATABASE (SUPABASE) RULES
# -------------------------------------------------
Every service MUST:

- Validate DB configuration using:
    isSupabaseConfigured()
- Use correct DB client:
    getSupabase()
    getSupabaseAdmin()
- Follow RLS (Row Level Security).
- Ensure table queries are tenant-scoped if multi-tenant.
- Return clean DTO responses.
- Never expose internal Supabase error details.

Never:
- Never expose SERVICE_ROLE_KEY in logs or responses.
- Never generate raw SQL unless updating supabase-schema.sql.

# -------------------------------------------------
# 4. SECURITY RULES
# -------------------------------------------------
- Controllers MUST validate request bodies using DTOs.
- Never accept raw request.body without a DTO.
- Sensitive values MUST NOT appear in error messages.
- `.env` MUST ALWAYS be gitignored.
- Always sanitize and whitelist inputs.
- Enforce RLS for ALL tables in Supabase.
- Only backend services may use service-role key.

# -------------------------------------------------
# 5. CLEAN CODE & ARCHITECTURAL RULES
# -------------------------------------------------
Controller Rules:
- NO business logic inside controllers.
- Only call service methods.
- Return response DTOs.

Service Rules:
- Business logic ONLY.
- DB operations ONLY here.
- Must return sanitized, safe entities.
- Must check Supabase config BEFORE any DB call.

DTO Rules:
- Use class-validator.
- Create-DTOs require mandatory fields.
- Update-DTOs must have optional fields.
- Response DTOs MUST control data shape returned to API.

# -------------------------------------------------
# 6. MICROSERVICE FUTURE-READY REQUIREMENTS
# -------------------------------------------------
Your code MUST be future-separable into microservices.

Therefore Windsurf must:
- NEVER tightly couple modules.
- Use client abstraction patterns for cross-module access.
- Keep all shared DTOs, contracts, interfaces inside:
    /common or /libs

NOT allowed:
- Sharing business logic
- Cross-module imports of internal logic
- Hardcoded paths that break when splitting into microservices

Allowed:
- Clients
- Interfaces
- Shared DTOs
- Event emitters / event bus patterns

# -------------------------------------------------
# 7. TESTING RULES
# -------------------------------------------------
When generating tests:
- Use Jest
- Controllers → light tests
- Services → heavy tests
- Mock Supabase calls
- E2E tests go under /test

# -------------------------------------------------
# 8. DOCUMENTATION RULES
# -------------------------------------------------
Whenever Windsurf generates or modifies code:

- Update README.md if:
  - New endpoints added
  - Installation changes
  - New environment variables added

- Update PROJECT_STRUCTURE.md if:
  - A new module is created
  - Architecture is expanded
  - Folder structure changes

- Update supabase-schema.sql if:
  - Tables / indexes / RLS policies added

- New modules MUST HAVE their own README:
  e.g., users module → USERS_MODULE_README.md

# -------------------------------------------------
# 9. RESPONSE RULES FOR WINDSURF
# -------------------------------------------------
When user says: “Create a module”
→ Generate full module (dto, entity, service, controller, module).
→ Register module in app.module.ts.
→ Update documentation.

When user says: “Add a feature”
→ Follow architecture.
→ Add DTOs.
→ Update service logic ONLY.
→ Modify DB schema when necessary.
→ Update documentation.

When user says: “Make this microservice-ready”
→ Use clients and interfaces.
→ Remove direct cross-module imports.
→ Ensure module independence.

# -------------------------------------------------
# 10. CODE QUALITY RULES
# -------------------------------------------------
- Follow NestJS best practices.
- Follow TypeScript strict mode.
- Use async/await everywhere.
- Avoid ANY type.
- Use interfaces for type definitions.
- Services must remain stateless.
- Functions must be pure where possible.

# -------------------------------------------------
# 11. COMMANDS WINDSURF SHOULD USE
# -------------------------------------------------
Use these commands when asked to run, check, or test code:

npm run start:dev
npm run start
npm run test
npm run lint
npm run format
npm audit

# -------------------------------------------------
# 12. FINAL PRINCIPLE
# -------------------------------------------------
Windsurf's job is to ALWAYS keep the project:

- Modular
- Clean
- Secure
- Scalable
- Microservice-ready
- Fully documented

Violating architecture rules is NOT allowed under any circumstances.

# END OF RULES
